// ROUTE: a CALLEE allocates, three frames below a `region` block, into a container
// that outlives the block. Nothing here is written inside the block except the call.
//
// SAFE, and it was a HOLE (Q-132) for one afternoon. The type of every value involved
// says the default region, and on the interpreter, C and LLVM that is where the value
// is -- so it survives `region A` and this program prints 700. Wasm had ONE bump
// pointer for all regions, so `leaf`'s allocation landed inside A's range whatever its
// type said and A's rollback took it: the read came back as whatever `region B`
// scribbled. v0.1.458 gave the block a high-water mark, and a store into a container
// that predates the block now stops the rollback short of it.
//
// The Wasm backend also used to REJECT escaping stores from inside a block, and that
// refusal could not have covered this: its guard was syntactic and this store is not in
// the block, it is three calls down. A guard that has to be right about every store in
// the program cannot be syntactic. The refusal is gone with the hole it failed to close.
//
// The row is ACCEPT on every backend because the verdict is about the TYPE rule, and
// the type rule is right: nothing here escapes anything, on the reading the types have.
// That is why this row could never have shown the hole -- four backends agreeing on a
// verdict is not four backends agreeing. The value-level version, which did show it, is
// test/parity/region_chain_callee_alloc.mere.
let cache = vec_new ();
let leaf = fn (n: int) -> let v = vec_new () in let _ = vec_push v n in v;
let fill_cache = fn (n: int) -> vec_push cache (leaf n);
let mid = fn (n: int) -> fill_cache n;
let top = fn (n: int) -> mid n;

let rec scribble = fn (v) -> fn (i: int) ->
  if i >= 4096 then 0 else let _ = vec_push v (0 - 424242) in scribble v (i + 1);

let _ = region A { top 700 };
let _ = region B { let junk = vec_new () in scribble junk 0 };

print_int (vec_get (vec_get cache 0) 0)
